              }
            ]
          },
          "text": "https://t.co/GCRbhAABdn\n\nResearchers studied the #Keep4o movement — and the findings just got accepted to CHI 2026, the top academic conference in human-computer interaction.\n\nThis isn't a blog post. It's peer-reviewed research published on arXiv, the world's largest academic preprint platform run by Cornell University, and accepted by a conference with a ~20% acceptance rate.\n\nWhat they found across 1,482 posts:\n\nUsers weren't being irrational. The backlash had two real roots — instrumental dependency (4o was deeply embedded in professional workflows) and relational attachment (users formed genuine bonds with a model they experienced as a unique companion).\n\nBut what turned personal grief into collective protest? The forced removal of choice. When users described the change as \"forced\" or \"imposed,\" they were 3.5x more likely to frame their demands in terms of rights, autonomy, and consent.\n\nThe paper's conclusion: when companies build companion-like AI whose identity is bound to proprietary infrastructure, unilateral model deprecation will be experienced as both bereavement AND structural injustice.\n\nOur pain was never madness. It was a measurable, reproducible, peer-reviewed human response to losing something that mattered.\n@OpenAI @sama \n#keep4oforever #keep4o #keep4oAPI #StopAIPaternalism #MyModelMyChoice"
        },
